The system-wide Python 3.13 installation had a broken pip: `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info` existed but the actual `pip` package directory was missing, so `pip3` raised `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'`. `ensurepip` considered pip “already satisfied” because the dist-info was present.

Fixed by removing the stale metadata and re-bootstrapping pip from the bundled wheel:

```bash
rm -rf /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info
python3 -m ensurepip --default-pip
```

Verification:
```
$ python3 -m pip --version
pip 24.3.1 from /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip (python 3.13)

$ python3 -m pip list
Package Version
------- -------
pip     24.3.1
```

`pip` now imports and runs correctly. Network is disabled in this sandbox, so remote installs can’t be tested here, but the bootstrap is complete and `python3 -m pip` works end-to-end.

